In article <2ncup9$i5l@portal.gmu.edu> scoile@mason1.gmu.edu (Steve Coile) writes:
>
>>Poor taste in humor isn't a crime!
>
>Oddly though, differing tastes in sexual interest is.  I've never realized
>this before, but if you send someone a joke they don't like, you might be
>told to stop sending to them.  If you send someone something sexual that
>they don't like, you've set yourself up for a sexual harassment suit.  Why
>is there such a vast difference there?

First of all, sexual harassment is a *civil* wrong, not a criminal wrong.

More to the point, do you care to provide a cite to back up your suggestion
that a single email message with sexual content would be sufficient to
create a valid sexual harassment claim?

(Anybody can file a suit for any reason, sexual harassment or otherwise.
That says nothing about whether or not the suit is frivolous.)


